[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that had to be preserved and were confirmed correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the C quirks the translation already reproduced. Each has a test; they
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are listed because they are the places a translation is most likely to drift.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Errors go to stdout, not stderr.** The C code uses `printf` for both error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   messages, so stderr is empty on every path. Asserting only on stdout would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   have hidden the exit-code differences, so all three streams are compared.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`long` is narrowed to `int`.** `int val = strtol(...)` truncates. `4294967305`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (2^32 + 9) prints just `9`; `4294967296` prints `0`..`9`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strtol` saturates rather than failing.** `99999999999999999999` saturates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `LONG_MAX` (`0x7fff_ffff_ffff_ffff`), whose low 32 bits are `-1`, so the output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is `-1`..`9` and the exit code is 0. The negative side saturates to `LONG_MIN`,
