[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   accepted and discarded: `5abc`, `1e5`, `3.9`, `0x10` (which parses `0` in base
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   10 and stops at `x`), `8,000` all succeed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strtol` skips leading C-locale whitespace** (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\r`) and an optional sign, but a string with no digits after that reports no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   conversion, so ` `, `\t`, `+`, `-`, `--5` and `  -  5` are all errors.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%` truncates toward zero in C.** `val % 10 == 9` is therefore never true for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a negative `val`: `-9` does not stop immediately, it counts all the way up to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   positive `9`. Rust's `%` has the same truncating semantics, so this maps
