The translation deliberately discards write errors (correct — the C never checks [38;5;10mprintf[0m's return), which is exactly what let the loop run to completion instead of dying. Fixed by resetting [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_DFL[0m at the top of [38;5;10mmain[0m, declaring libc's [38;5;10msignal()[0m directly rather than adding a crate dependency.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Everything else in the translation was already faithful, including the parts that look like bugs: errors printed to stdout rather than stderr, [38;5;10mlong[0m→[38;5;10mint[0m truncation ([38;5;10m4294967305[0m prints just [38;5;10m9[0m), [38;5;10mstrtol[0m saturating to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m instead of erroring, only [38;5;10mend == argv[1][0m being checked so trailing garbage is accepted, C's truncating [38;5;10m%[0m meaning negative values never stop early ([38;5;10m-9[0m counts all the way up to [38;5;10m9[0m), and [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m wrapping to [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m.[0m[0m
